.Dd $Mdocdate: October 27 2026 $
.Dt FUGUPASS 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm fugupass
.Nd FuguPass vault directory
.Sh DESCRIPTION
This page describes each file of a vault directory of
.Xr fugupass 1 .
Each path below sits inside the vault directory.
.Bl -tag -width "machine/wrap.index.i" -compact
.It Pa index
the sealed index of the vault
.It Pa machine/canary.\& Ns Ar i
the canary check seal of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/change
the marker of an incomplete passphrase change or threshold change
.It Pa machine/config
the config file of this machine, plaintext
.It Pa machine/counters
the last counter of each record of this machine
.It Pa machine/factor
the device factor of this machine
.It Pa machine/wrap.index.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's index wrap of oracle
.Ar i
.It Pa machine/wrap.\& Ns Ar e Ns Pa \&.\& Ns Ar i
this machine's wrap of slot
.Ar e
at oracle
.Ar i
.El
.Pp
The sealed file of one entry, and the sealed file of one free slot,
each take the lowercase hex of the hash of the slot key as the file
name.
A listing of the vault directory therefore names no entry.
.Ss The config file
The config file is plaintext, and it holds no secret.
A copy of it leaks the oracle list, the threshold, the machine name,
the plate check value, and the tunables.
A holder of the plate check value can confirm that a candidate plate
belongs to this vault.
.Ss The counters file
The counters file holds the last counter of each record of this
machine, and it holds no secret.
The next request of a record sends the wall clock or the stored
value plus one, whichever is higher.
The loss of the file is therefore safe.
Three residual risks remain.
A machine with a stale clock and a lost counters file sends a counter
below the stored one.
The oracle then answers junk, and it burns no strike.
A junk answer names no cause: a low counter, a wrong passphrase, and
a wiped record give the same bytes.
An envelope captured before a re-enrollment replays until the stored
counter passes its value.
.Ss Copies between machines
FuguPass defines no sync protocol and no write coordination between
machines.
Two machines that create an entry at the same time can consume one
slot twice.
A later file copy then overwrites the file of one entry.
A refill on a machine with a stale index can reserve the slot indexes
that a refill of another machine reserved.
Create each entry and run each refill on one machine,
the minting machine.
Copy the shared set from that machine to every other machine.
